+\section[W+jets estimation]{$\wjets$ estimation}
+\label{sec:ch8_wjets}
+
+\subsection{Control-region definition}
+\label{subsec:ch8_wcr_definition}
+
+The $\CRW$ regions select $\wlnu$ events by replacing the $\tau$ in the corresponding $\bVetoSR$ selection with an electron or muon.
+The $b$-jet veto suppresses top-quark production and provides a larger $\wjets$ sample than a tagged selection.
+Exactly one signal electron or muon and no selected $\tau$ are required.
+The signal-lepton requirements are those in Chapter~\ref{chapter:object_reconstruction}.
+Single-lepton triggers are used, as listed in Section~\ref{sec:analysis_triggers}.
+This choice avoids the different response of the calorimeter-based $\met$ trigger to electrons and muons, since the trigger-level missing momentum does not include the muon momentum.
+
+The lepton replaces the $\tau$ in the transverse mass and angular separation, giving $\mT^\ell$ and $\DphiLepMet$ through the definitions in Section~\ref{subsec:ch7_variables}.
+The leading jet is used for the resonant/non-resonant categorisation and the invariant mass $m_{j\ell}$.
+Table~\ref{tab:ch8_wcr_vrw} gives the selections together with those of $\VRb$.
+The kinematic correspondence to the SRs restricts the extrapolation to similar hard-scattering configurations.
+The neutrino from a hadronic $\tau$ decay changes both the visible $\tau$ momentum and $\mpt$, so the lepton replacement does not imply identical reconstructed distributions.
+These differences are retained in the simulated acceptance ratio.
+The extrapolation is checked in the lower-mass $\wtaunu$ sample described in Section~\ref{subsec:ch8_tau1b_vr}.
+Both CRs have a $\wjets$ purity of approximately $70\%$.
+Diboson production is the largest secondary contribution in $\CRW$-$\Res$.
