+\subsection{Electron--muon consistency}
+\label{subsec:ch8_emu}
+
+The electron and muon channels give pre-fit data/MC ratios differing by $1.4\sigma$ in $\CRW$-$\Res$ and $1.7\sigma$ in $\CRW$-$\NonRes$.
+The non-resonant difference is investigated through the corrections applied to simulation, the selection cutflow and the leading-jet $\pT$ distribution.
+The lepton-dependent efficiency corrections, including the trigger scale factors, are checked separately in the two channels, with no incorrect application identified.
+The difference becomes visible after imposing $50<\pT^{j_1}<250~\GeV$.
+
+Figure~\ref{fig:ch8_emu_comparison} compares the electron and muon distributions separately in data and simulation.
+The electron data have an upward fluctuation around $\pT^{j_1}=80$--$100~\GeV$.
+The simulated event-weight distributions reveal a second effect: a small number of events carry weights of order 30.
+Most occur below $50~\GeV$, but one such event enters the selected muon sample above this threshold, with no corresponding event in the electron sample.
+A single simulated event can therefore increase the predicted muon yield by several tens of weighted events and lower its data/MC ratio.
+
+These observations are consistent with statistical fluctuations in the electron data and the weighted muon simulation.
+They do not establish a lepton-dependent correction to the $\wjets$ rate.
+The two channels are therefore combined within each CR, and one $\wjets$ normalisation factor is used for each topology.
+The extrapolation of this combined constraint is checked in the VRs described in Section~\ref{sec:ch8_validation}.
+
