+\section[Fake-tau estimation]{Fake-$\tau$ estimation}
+\label{sec:ch8_fake}
+
+The fake-$\tau$ contribution is small because the selected SM background is dominated by events containing a genuine hadronically decaying $\tau$.
+As discussed in Section~\ref{sec:ch7_sr1b}, its principal source at large $\met$ is $\znunu$+jets, where a jet passes the $\tau$ identification.
+This contribution is at most about $10\%$ of the background in the SRs considered in the fake-rate study and is much smaller in the one-$b$-jet SRs.
+The nominal yield is taken from simulation, and a dedicated $\zll$ study determines an uncertainty for the modelling of jets misidentified as $\tau$ candidates.
+
+\subsection[Validation with Z dilepton events]{Validation with $\zll$ events}
+\label{subsec:ch8_zvr}
+
+The $Z$ validation region (ZVR) selects a visible $Z$ decay together with a $\tau$ candidate to test the simulated fake rate in a sample enriched in $\zjets$.
+Two signal electrons or two signal muons are required, with $75<m_{\ell\ell}<105~\GeV$, together with exactly one $\tau$ candidate and a $b$-jet veto.
+Events are required to satisfy $1\leq N_j\leq3$ and $\pT^\tau>100~\GeV$.
+Both lepton channels are used to increase the statistical precision.
+In the muon channel, events are selected using the $\met$ trigger, whereas a single-electron trigger is used in the electron channel.
+
+The quantity $|\vec p_{\mathrm{T}}^{\,\ell\ell}+\mpt|$ is required to exceed $200~\GeV$.
+Adding the reconstructed dilepton momentum to $\mpt$ treats the visible leptons as unobserved and provides a recoil quantity analogous to the missing momentum in $\znunu$ events.
+An additional $\pT^{\ell\ell}>130~\GeV$ requirement suppresses top-quark contamination.
+A transverse-mass threshold of $100~\GeV$ is also applied in this study.
+The common multijet requirement is retained.
+
+A total of 116 data events pass the ZVR selection, and the simulated $\zjets$ purity is approximately $90\%$.
+Figure~\ref{fig:ch8_zvr_pt} shows the $\pT^\tau$ distribution with its threshold removed.
+The simulation overestimates the yield at high $\pT^\tau$.
+Differences of up to $60\%$ are observed in the overall yields in the validation comparisons.
+This comparison alone is sensitive to both the fake rate and the production kinematics of $\zjets$.
+The RNN-score distributions in Figure~\ref{fig:ch8_zvr_rnn} provide a further check using looser $\tau$ identification and a relaxed transverse-mass requirement.
+They compare samples with $\pT^\tau>20~\GeV$ and $\pT^\tau>100~\GeV$.
+The better agreement at low RNN scores indicates that the discrepancy is mainly connected to the probability for a jet to pass the $\tau$ identification.
+
+The small SR contribution and the precision of this check do not motivate a differential correction of the simulated fake rate.
+The nominal MC prediction for $\znunu$+jets is therefore retained with an additional $100\%$ normalisation uncertainty.
+This uncertainty covers the discrepancy observed in the ZVR.
+Changing the assigned uncertainty from $50\%$ to $100\%$ gives no appreciable change in the tested final results because this background is small.
+This additional uncertainty describes the fake-$\tau$ modelling in this component and is separate from the uncertainties in genuine-$\tau$ reconstruction and identification.
+No independent fake-factor prediction is used for the SR yield.
+
